{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Not by your righteousness.</b> That is, it is not by your righteousness that you are worthy to possess the land; but in any case the generation that was to enter the land was a worthy generation, and so you find that He said to them ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״ and so forth (Deuteronomy 4:4) — only they were not complete enough for the land to be given them on their own merit if not for the covenant of the fathers. And although the covenant of the fathers would not have availed to possess the land if they had been wicked — and the generation of the wilderness proves it — as the Raavad wrote in his commentary on the Mishnah, in chapter 2 of Eduyyot, where we learned: the father merits for the son, and so forth, and in the numbering of the generations before Him — and this is the end, as it says ״קוֹרֵא הַדּוֹרוֹת מֵרֹאשׁ״ (Isaiah 41:4), end quote. And the Raavad wrote, and this is his language: that He sees the generation that is worthy and rolls along with them until that generation, and so forth — like the generation of Joshua, and so forth, end quote. This shows that a worthy generation is also required. Nevertheless, their merit did not contain the measure by which they would deserve to inherit the land if not for the covenant of the fathers. Accordingly, the verse's saying <b>not by your righteousness</b> and so forth means: even though you are a complete congregation, there is no power in your merit to attain this thing. And He was emphatic in saying ״לֹא בְצִדְקָתְךָ״ to indicate that their righteousness did not avail even to assist, for the covenant of the fathers suffices; rather, what their good deeds accomplished was to ensure that the good would not be withheld from them." "<b>For the wickedness of… and in order to… which He swore….</b> And He did not suffice with only one of these, for without the wickedness of the nations HaShem would not have dispossessed them — not in accordance with justice — and by the wickedness alone Israel would not be worthy to take their land. Or it is possible that the meaning of ״מוֹרִישָׁם״ is to destroy them, and therefore wickedness was necessary, for without the wickedness HaShem would not have decreed upon them (Deuteronomy 20:16) ״לֹא תְחַיֶּה כָּל נְשָׁמָה״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:23.097889370Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}